  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/b2c3640581329789441015f744b3dd4fc06f5178a9044a844eefc1b8616f4ea4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-3))?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}=4$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/8a427e6cf526ce00e227e5ffeae3a21bab23fe17d1f427c8419fe3c894a761b5.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/cdaaa955e3848d06106737098260b218752aa2a82a7aa060435ff311ced57f34.png)",
